    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Source separation, the process of separating urine and feces at the toilet, is gaining attention for its potential to recover macronutrients (N,P,K), and lower the global greenhouse-gas footprint, and reduce environmental pollution. Urine contains 80% of the total nitrogen load of wastewater streams, but constitutes only 1% of the total volume. The extraction of nitrogen directly from urine could eliminate the need for nitrification and denitrification processes, and therefore reduce the overall energy demand of conventional wastewater treatment. Nitrogen (in the form of ammonium) can be extracted from source-separated urine via cation exchange and acid elution, and is readily available for plant uptake.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
